  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at half height. It is steeper along x, so overshoot shows there first. With no noise, steps diverge when learning rate × 3 > 2, so above about 0.67.",
    "Each step moves by −learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise is Gaussian, centred on zero, size 2·√((64−B)/(63·B)) for batch B of 64 points, so it is exactly zero at 64.",
    "Both arrows are steps (rate × slope estimate), drawn at 2.5× their true length on one scale. Purple: the mini-batch step the ball takes next. Dark: the step the full data would give.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 6, the loss at the bowl's edge along x (diverged). The trail holds the start point plus one point per step.",
    "Noise comes from the fixed seed, run number and step, so Reset replays the same sequence. A new run uses different noise. Off-bowl points and the ball are drawn at the rim; the loss readout keeps the true value."
  ]
}